    X - handle forms (rotom-wash, urshifu-rapid-strike, etc.) - these trip up name matching

reduce pokeapi calls to target any data not obtained from pokedex reference
    - parseInput currently calls pokeapi for every pokemon, every move, and every item
    - moves table: name, type, power, priority, damage class
    - items table: name, sprite url
    - new endpoint so the frontend gets pokemon/move/item details from the backend in one request
    - fall back to pokeapi only for anything missing, and save the result for next time
    - special function to handle arceus cases
    - has differnt form links in pokeapi query

X store pokedex in postgres

map trends over time (this will involve expanding postgres to hold more tables, which will in turn increase hosting costs if and when)
    possible solution is to extract only the rank name tier and date over the last 12 months [this is still an enormous amount of data]
    - rough sizing: ~50 tiers x ~500 pokemon x 12 months = ~300,000 rows
    - with narrow columns (pokemon id, tier id, month, rank, usage %) that's tens of MB, not GB
    - store pokemon/tier as integer ids that reference the pokedex/tiers tables, not repeated text
    - only keep pokemon above some usage cutoff per tier to shrink it further
    - index on (tier, pokemon, month) for the "one pokemon over time" chart
    - a monthly import job to add the newest month (and optionally drop anything older than 12 months)
    - chart on the frontend (line chart of rank or usage % per month)

other data sciencey things, likelihood of teammates and such
    leans on chaos[teammates] section
    start out with just listing chaos information like top 10 moves, top 8 teammates, top 5 held items and so on, with %ages
    will need its own page [Explore?]
    - check exactly how smogon calculates each chaos section before turning numbers into %ages
      (some sections aren't simple counts, so dividing by total usage may not give what you expect)
    - store per (pokemon, tier, month): top moves, items, abilities, teammates, spreads
        option A: one JSONB column per section (quick to build)
        option B: separate narrow tables (better if you want to query across pokemon later)
    - endpoint: /apiv1/explore/<tier>/<pokemon>
    - explore page: search box, pokemon card, sections for moves/items/teammates/etc. with % bars
    - later: "suggest a teammate" for a team being built, based on the teammates data

